const windowsSecurityArchitectureComponentsLessonContent = "# Windows Security: Windows Operating System Architecture and Security Components\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to:\n\n1. Explain the architecture of the Windows operating system.\n2. Differentiate between user mode and kernel mode.\n3. Understand the Windows kernel and its responsibilities.\n4. Explain how Windows manages processes, threads and memory.\n5. Understand the Windows Registry and its security importance.\n6. Explain Windows security architecture and the Security Reference Monitor.\n7. Understand Security Identifiers, access tokens and access control lists.\n8. Explain Windows authentication and authorization mechanisms.\n9. Identify important Windows security services and components.\n10. Inspect Windows architecture and security components using PowerShell and other built-in tools.\n\n---\n\n# PART 1: WINDOWS OPERATING SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE\n\n## 1.1 What Is Windows Operating System Architecture?\n\nWindows operating system architecture refers to the internal organization of Windows and how its different components work together to manage computer hardware, applications, memory, files, users and security.\n\nAn operating system acts as an intermediary between applications and computer hardware.\n\nFor example, when you open a browser, the browser needs access to:\n\n- Processor resources.\n- RAM.\n- Storage.\n- Network interfaces.\n- Display hardware.\n- Audio devices.\n\nApplications do not normally control these hardware resources directly. Windows provides controlled interfaces that allow applications to request services from the operating system.\n\n### Basic operating system architecture\n\n```text\n             USER\n              |\n              v\n       APPLICATIONS\n              |\n              v\n       WINDOWS APIs\n              |\n              v\n       OPERATING SYSTEM\n              |\n       +------+------+\n       |             |\n       v             v\n     KERNEL       DRIVERS\n       |             |\n       +------+------+\n              |\n              v\n           HARDWARE\n              |\n     +--------+---------+\n     |        |         |\n    CPU      RAM      STORAGE\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n**User:** The person interacting with the computer.\n\n**Applications:** Programs such as browsers, text editors, games and business applications.\n\n**Windows APIs:** Interfaces that applications use to request operating system services.\n\n**Kernel:** The core of the operating system, responsible for essential system operations.\n\n**Drivers:** Software components that allow Windows to communicate with hardware devices.\n\n**Hardware:** The physical components of the computer.\n\nThis architecture makes it possible for applications to use hardware without independently managing every hardware detail.\n\n## 1.2 The Main Layers of Windows Architecture\n\nWindows architecture can be understood through several major layers.\n\n| Layer | Main Responsibility | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| User applications | Perform tasks for users. | Chrome, Word, Visual Studio |\n| User-mode system components | Provide operating system services and interfaces. | Windows services, system processes |\n| Windows APIs | Allow applications to request system functionality. | Win32 API |\n| Kernel-mode components | Manage core operating system functions. | Kernel, Executive, drivers |\n| Hardware abstraction | Provides hardware-independent interfaces. | Hardware Abstraction Layer |\n| Physical hardware | Executes instructions and stores information. | CPU, RAM, SSD |\n\nThese layers are conceptual. Actual Windows architecture includes many interacting components rather than a simple linear stack.\n\n## 1.3 User Mode and Kernel Mode\n\nOne of the most important concepts in Windows architecture is the separation between user mode and kernel mode.\n\nWindows uses processor privilege levels to separate ordinary applications from highly privileged operating system components.\n\n### A. User Mode\n\nUser mode is where ordinary applications and many system processes execute.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Web browsers.\n- Media players.\n- Text editors.\n- Office applications.\n- Many Windows services and background processes.\n\nUser-mode applications have restricted access to hardware and protected operating system memory.\n\nWhen an application needs an operating system service, it requests that service through an approved system interface.\n\n### B. Kernel Mode\n\nKernel mode is a highly privileged execution mode.\n\nImportant Windows kernel-mode components include:\n\n- The Windows kernel.\n- The Executive.\n- Device drivers.\n- Other kernel-mode system components.\n\nKernel-mode components can access protected system memory and interact with hardware through privileged mechanisms.\n\nA serious error in kernel mode can affect the entire operating system.\n\nFor example, a faulty kernel driver may cause a system crash.\n\n### User mode vs. kernel mode\n\n| Feature | User Mode | Kernel Mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| Privilege | Restricted | Highly privileged |\n| Memory access | Restricted to permitted address space | Can access kernel memory |\n| Hardware access | Through approved interfaces | Directly through privileged mechanisms |\n| Typical components | Applications and many services | Kernel and drivers |\n| Failure impact | Usually limited to the process | Can crash or compromise the system |\n| Security risk | Application compromise | Potential system-wide compromise |\n\n### Architecture diagram\n\n```text\n             USER MODE\n    +---------------------------+\n    |                           |\n    | Browser                   |\n    | Office Applications       |\n    | Business Applications     |\n    | User-mode Services        |\n    |                           |\n    +-------------+-------------+\n                  |\n             System Calls\n                  |\n    +-------------v-------------+\n    |                           |\n    |        KERNEL MODE        |\n    |                           |\n    | Windows Kernel            |\n    | Executive                 |\n    | Device Drivers            |\n    |                           |\n    +-------------+-------------+\n                  |\n                  v\n              HARDWARE\n```\n\n### Why is this separation important?\n\nImagine a browser contains a programming error.\n\nIf the browser operates in user mode, Windows can usually isolate the failure to that application.\n\nHowever, if a malicious or vulnerable kernel driver is compromised, the attacker may gain much greater control over the operating system.\n\nThis is why kernel-mode drivers require particular security attention.\n\n## 1.4 The Windows Kernel\n\nThe Windows kernel is the central component responsible for low-level operating system operations.\n\nIt works with the Windows Executive and other kernel-mode components to manage system resources.\n\n### Major responsibilities\n\n**1. Process and thread scheduling**\n\nThe operating system decides which threads receive processor time.\n\n**2. Interrupt handling**\n\nThe kernel responds to hardware and software interrupts.\n\n**3. Synchronization**\n\nIt coordinates access to shared resources so that different threads do not interfere with one another.\n\n**4. Low-level processor management**\n\nIt handles processor-specific operations required by the operating system.\n\n**5. Kernel-mode execution**\n\nIt provides the foundation for privileged system operations.\n\n### What is a system call?\n\nA system call is a controlled mechanism through which a user-mode program requests a service from the operating system.\n\nFor example, a program may need to:\n\n- Read a file.\n- Create a process.\n- Allocate memory.\n- Access a device.\n- Communicate with another process.\n\nThe application requests the service through an operating system interface.\n\n### Simplified system-call process\n\n```text\n       USER APPLICATION\n               |\n               v\n       Requests File Access\n               |\n               v\n       WINDOWS API FUNCTION\n               |\n               v\n        SYSTEM CALL\n               |\n               v\n       KERNEL-MODE SERVICE\n               |\n               v\n       ACCESS CHECKS\n               |\n          +----+----+\n          |         |\n        Allowed   Denied\n          |         |\n          v         v\n       Operation  Error Result\n       Performed\n```\n\nWindows performs the necessary checks before allowing protected operations.\n\nFor example, a process attempting to access a protected file may be denied if its security token does not grant the required access.\n\n## 1.5 Windows Executive\n\nThe Windows Executive is a collection of kernel-mode components responsible for higher-level operating system services.\n\nImportant Executive components include:\n\n| Component | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Object Manager | Manages Windows kernel objects. |\n| Process Manager | Manages processes and threads. |\n| Memory Manager | Manages virtual memory and physical memory allocation. |\n| I/O Manager | Coordinates input/output operations. |\n| Security Reference Monitor | Enforces access-control decisions. |\n| Configuration Manager | Manages the Windows Registry. |\n| Cache Manager | Supports file-system caching. |\n| Plug and Play Manager | Coordinates device installation and configuration. |\n| Power Manager | Manages power-related operations. |\n\nThese components work together to provide operating system functionality.\n\n### Example: Opening a file\n\nImagine a user opens a document stored on an SSD.\n\nA simplified process looks like this:\n\n```text\n       USER OPENS DOCUMENT\n                |\n                v\n       APPLICATION REQUEST\n                |\n                v\n         WINDOWS API\n                |\n                v\n          I/O MANAGER\n                |\n                v\n       SECURITY CHECKS\n                |\n                v\n         FILE SYSTEM\n                |\n                v\n         STORAGE DRIVER\n                |\n                v\n              SSD\n                |\n                v\n        DOCUMENT DATA\n```\n\nThe actual process involves additional components, but this diagram shows the important relationship between application requests, access checks, I/O handling and hardware communication.\n\n## 1.6 Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL)\n\nThe Hardware Abstraction Layer provides an interface that helps Windows operate across supported hardware platforms without requiring every operating system component to understand every hardware-specific detail.\n\nFor example, Windows needs to interact with:\n\n- Interrupt controllers.\n- Timers.\n- Processor-related hardware facilities.\n- Other platform-specific components.\n\nThe HAL helps isolate certain hardware-specific differences.\n\nThis makes operating system development and hardware compatibility easier.\n\n## 1.7 Windows Device Drivers\n\nA device driver is software that allows Windows to communicate with a hardware device or provide a software-defined device interface.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Graphics drivers.\n- Network adapter drivers.\n- Audio drivers.\n- Storage drivers.\n- Printer drivers.\n- USB device drivers.\n\n### How drivers work\n\nImagine connecting a network adapter to a computer.\n\nWindows needs a driver that allows the operating system to communicate with the adapter.\n\n```text\n        WINDOWS APPLICATION\n                |\n                v\n         WINDOWS NETWORK\n             SERVICES\n                |\n                v\n        NETWORK INTERFACE\n             DRIVER\n                |\n                v\n          NETWORK ADAPTER\n                |\n                v\n             NETWORK\n```\n\n### Why drivers are security-sensitive\n\nA vulnerable driver can provide an attacker with opportunities to gain elevated privileges.\n\nAttackers may attempt to abuse legitimate but vulnerable drivers to bypass certain security controls.\n\nThis is one reason Windows supports security features such as:\n\n- Driver signing.\n- Vulnerable driver blocklists.\n- Memory integrity on supported systems.\n- Secure Boot.\n\n### Driver signing\n\nDriver signing uses digital signatures to help Windows establish the origin and integrity of driver packages.\n\nIt does not mean every signed driver is automatically safe.\n\nA legitimately signed driver may still contain vulnerabilities.\n\n## 1.8 Windows Processes and Threads\n\nA process is an instance of a running program.\n\nA thread is an execution unit scheduled by the operating system within a process.\n\nFor example, when you open a browser:\n\n- Windows creates a process or multiple related processes.\n- Those processes contain threads.\n- Threads perform different operations.\n- The processor schedules threads to execute.\n\n### Process and thread relationship\n\n```text\n             BROWSER PROCESS\n                    |\n          +---------+---------+\n          |         |         |\n          v         v         v\n       Thread 1  Thread 2  Thread 3\n          |         |         |\n          v         v         v\n       Interface  Network   Background\n       Activity   Activity  Processing\n```\n\nModern browsers may use multiple processes for tabs, extensions, rendering and other functions.\n\n### Why processes matter in cybersecurity\n\nMalware often runs as a process.\n\nA security analyst may investigate:\n\n- Unknown processes.\n- Suspicious parent-child relationships.\n- Unusual executable locations.\n- Unexpected network activity.\n- Unusual process privileges.\n\nA process name alone is not sufficient to identify malware.\n\n## 1.9 Windows Memory Management\n\nWindows uses virtual memory to manage how applications access memory.\n\nEach process normally operates within its own virtual address space.\n\nThis provides isolation between processes and helps prevent one application from directly accessing another application's protected memory.\n\n### Virtual memory concept\n\n```text\n          PROCESS A\n              |\n              v\n       VIRTUAL ADDRESS\n            SPACE\n              |\n              v\n        MEMORY MANAGER\n              |\n              v\n     PHYSICAL MEMORY (RAM)\n              |\n              v\n        MEMORY HARDWARE\n```\n\nWindows translates virtual addresses into physical memory locations using processor-supported memory-management mechanisms.\n\n### Important memory concepts\n\n| Concept | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| Virtual memory | Address space presented to a process. |\n| Physical memory | Actual RAM installed in the computer. |\n| Paging | Moving memory pages between RAM and backing storage when needed. |\n| Page table | Data structure used to translate virtual addresses. |\n| Memory protection | Restricts how processes access memory. |\n| DEP | Helps prevent execution of code from certain memory regions. |\n| ASLR | Randomizes important memory locations to make exploitation harder. |\n\n### Data Execution Prevention (DEP)\n\nDEP helps prevent code execution from memory regions that are intended to contain data rather than executable instructions.\n\nThis can make certain memory-corruption attacks harder to exploit.\n\n### Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR)\n\nASLR makes memory addresses less predictable by randomizing the locations of important program and system components.\n\nThis makes it harder for an attacker to rely on fixed memory addresses when exploiting certain vulnerabilities.\n\nNeither DEP nor ASLR guarantees that software is free from vulnerabilities.\n\n## 1.10 Windows File System Architecture\n\nWindows supports several file systems, including:\n\n- NTFS.\n- FAT32.\n- exFAT.\n- ReFS in supported Windows environments.\n\nNTFS is widely used for Windows system and data volumes.\n\n### NTFS security capabilities\n\nNTFS supports:\n\n- File and folder permissions.\n- Access Control Lists.\n- File ownership.\n- Auditing.\n- Encryption through Encrypting File System (EFS), where supported.\n- Alternate Data Streams.\n- File-system metadata and journaling.\n\n### Understanding file permissions\n\nSuppose a company stores financial reports in:\n\n```text\nC:\\Company\\Finance\\\n```\n\nThe company may want:\n\n- Accountants to read and modify the reports.\n- Managers to read the reports.\n- Ordinary employees to have no access.\n\nNTFS permissions can help enforce these restrictions.\n\n### Part 1 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- The structure of Windows architecture.\n- User mode and kernel mode.\n- Windows kernel responsibilities.\n- Windows Executive components.\n- System calls.\n- Hardware Abstraction Layer.\n- Device drivers.\n- Processes and threads.\n- Memory management.\n- NTFS file-system security.\n\n**Progress: Part 1 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 2: WINDOWS SECURITY ARCHITECTURE AND SECURITY COMPONENTS\n\n## 2.1 Understanding Windows Security Architecture\n\nWindows security architecture refers to the mechanisms Windows uses to identify users, authenticate identities, control access to resources and protect the operating system.\n\nIt includes:\n\n- Security identifiers.\n- Access tokens.\n- Security descriptors.\n- Access Control Lists.\n- Security Reference Monitor.\n- Local Security Authority.\n- Authentication packages.\n- Credential protection.\n- Security policies.\n\nThese components work together to determine whether an operation is allowed.\n\n## 2.2 Security Identifiers (SIDs)\n\nA Security Identifier (SID) is a unique value used by Windows to identify a security principal.\n\nSecurity principals include:\n\n- User accounts.\n- Groups.\n- Computer accounts.\n- Certain service identities.\n\nWindows uses SIDs internally rather than relying only on account names.\n\n### Example SID\n\n```text\nS-1-5-21-123456789-987654321-123456789-1001\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative SID.\n\nIts general structure contains:\n\n| Section | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| S | Identifies the value as a SID. |\n| 1 | SID revision. |\n| 5 | Identifier authority. |\n| Remaining values | Subauthority values and the account identifier. |\n\nThe final relative identifier (RID) commonly distinguishes accounts within a local or domain security authority.\n\n### Why are SIDs important?\n\nImagine an employee changes their username.\n\nThe account's SID generally remains the identity used by Windows to represent that security principal.\n\nThis allows permissions and security references to remain associated with the account rather than depending solely on the displayed username.\n\n### Inspecting your SID\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /user\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nUSER INFORMATION\n\nUser Name       SID\n--------------- ---------------------------------------\nDESKTOP-01\\brian S-1-5-21-123456789-987654321-123456789-1001\n```\n\nYour actual SID will be different.\n\n## 2.3 Access Tokens\n\nAn access token is a security object associated with a process or thread that describes its security context.\n\nIt contains information used during access-control decisions.\n\nImportant information includes:\n\n- User SID.\n- Group SIDs.\n- Privileges.\n- Logon session information.\n- Other security-related attributes.\n\n### How access tokens work\n\nWhen a user signs in, Windows creates a logon session and a corresponding security token.\n\nWhen the user starts an application, the process receives a primary access token.\n\nWindows uses the token when evaluating access to protected resources.\n\n### Access token diagram\n\n```text\n              USER SIGN-IN\n                   |\n                   v\n             AUTHENTICATION\n                   |\n                   v\n            SECURITY TOKEN\n                   |\n       +-----------+-----------+\n       |           |           |\n       v           v           v\n     User SID   Group SIDs  Privileges\n       |           |           |\n       +-----------+-----------+\n                   |\n                   v\n             NEW PROCESS\n                   |\n                   v\n            ACCESS REQUEST\n                   |\n                   v\n             ACCESS CHECK\n```\n\n### Why are access tokens important?\n\nSuppose a standard user tries to modify a protected Windows system file.\n\nWindows examines the process's security context and the permissions associated with the file.\n\nIf the token does not provide the necessary access, Windows denies the operation.\n\n### Inspecting privileges\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /priv\n```\n\nThis displays privileges associated with your current logon token.\n\nSome privileges may be present but disabled until enabled by an authorized process.\n\nDo not assume that a privilege listed in the output is automatically active.\n\n## 2.4 Security Descriptors\n\nA security descriptor is a data structure that describes the security information associated with a protected Windows object.\n\nIt can contain:\n\n- Owner information.\n- Primary group information.\n- Discretionary Access Control List (DACL).\n- System Access Control List (SACL).\n\n### Security descriptor structure\n\n```text\n             SECURITY DESCRIPTOR\n                      |\n       +--------------+--------------+\n       |              |              |\n       v              v              v\n     OWNER          DACL            SACL\n       |              |              |\n       v              v              v\n   Identifies     Controls        Supports\n   ownership      permissions    auditing\n```\n\n### Owner\n\nIdentifies the security principal that owns the object.\n\n### DACL\n\nDefines which users and groups are allowed or denied access.\n\n### SACL\n\nDefines auditing information for access to an object.\n\nThe system can generate audit events when configured access conditions occur.\n\n## 2.5 Access Control Lists (ACLs)\n\nWindows uses Access Control Lists to control access to files, folders and other securable objects.\n\nThere are two important types.\n\n### A. Discretionary Access Control List (DACL)\n\nA DACL determines which security principals are allowed or denied access.\n\nIt contains Access Control Entries (ACEs).\n\nAn ACE can specify:\n\n- A user or group SID.\n- Allowed permissions.\n- Denied permissions.\n- Other access-control details.\n\n### Example\n\nImagine the folder:\n\n```text\nC:\\Company\\Finance\\\n```\n\nIts permissions might be:\n\n| Account or Group | Permission |\n|---|---|\n| Administrators | Full control |\n| Finance Team | Modify |\n| Managers | Read and execute |\n| General Employees | No access |\n\nThis is an example of access control based on organizational roles.\n\n### B. System Access Control List (SACL)\n\nA SACL defines which access events should be audited.\n\nFor example, an administrator may configure auditing to record attempts to access sensitive financial documents.\n\nThis helps investigate:\n\n- Unauthorized access attempts.\n- Changes to protected files.\n- Suspicious account activity.\n- Attempts to modify sensitive information.\n\n### DACL vs. SACL\n\n| DACL | SACL |\n|---|---|\n| Controls access. | Defines auditing conditions. |\n| Allows or denies permissions. | Specifies which access events should be audited. |\n| Used for authorization. | Used for security monitoring. |\n\n## 2.6 Security Reference Monitor (SRM)\n\nThe Security Reference Monitor is a Windows kernel-mode security component responsible for enforcing access-control decisions.\n\nIt works with security information associated with processes and protected objects.\n\n### Simplified access-control process\n\n```text\n          PROCESS\n             |\n             v\n       ACCESS TOKEN\n             |\n             v\n       ACCESS REQUEST\n             |\n             v\n      SECURITY REFERENCE\n          MONITOR\n             |\n             v\n       CHECK SECURITY\n         DESCRIPTOR\n             |\n        +----+----+\n        |         |\n        v         v\n      Allow     Deny\n        |         |\n        v         v\n     Resource  Access\n     Granted   Rejected\n```\n\nFor example, a process requests permission to read a file.\n\nWindows checks the process's security token against the file's security descriptor.\n\nIf the requested access is permitted, the operation can proceed.\n\nOtherwise, access is denied.\n\nThe actual implementation involves several Windows components, but this model explains the fundamental access-control process.\n\n## 2.7 Local Security Authority (LSA)\n\nThe Local Security Authority is responsible for important local security functions.\n\nIt supports:\n\n- Local security policy enforcement.\n- Authentication-related operations.\n- Security auditing.\n- Management of security information.\n- Coordination with authentication packages.\n\nThe Local Security Authority Subsystem Service runs in the `lsass.exe` process.\n\n### What is LSASS?\n\nLSASS stands for Local Security Authority Subsystem Service.\n\nIt is a critical Windows security process.\n\nIts responsibilities include supporting authentication and enforcing local security policies.\n\n### Why is LSASS security-sensitive?\n\nLSASS processes sensitive authentication information.\n\nIf attackers gain access to sensitive credentials or authentication material handled by LSASS, they may attempt to compromise other accounts or systems.\n\nThis is why Windows includes credential-protection mechanisms such as:\n\n- Credential Guard on supported systems.\n- LSASS protection through protected process mechanisms where configured.\n- Restricted administrative access.\n- Endpoint security monitoring.\n\n### Inspecting LSASS\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Process lsass\n```\n\nThis checks whether the LSASS process is running.\n\nYou may need administrator permissions to see certain process information.\n\n**Important:** LSASS is a legitimate and critical Windows process. Do not terminate it.\n\n## 2.8 Windows Authentication Components\n\nWindows supports multiple authentication mechanisms.\n\nCommon mechanisms include:\n\n### A. NTLM\n\nNTLM is a Windows authentication protocol family that uses challenge-response mechanisms.\n\nIt has historically been used for authentication in Windows environments.\n\nHowever, it has limitations and is generally less desirable than Kerberos for modern domain authentication where Kerberos is available.\n\n### B. Kerberos\n\nKerberos is a network authentication protocol commonly used in Active Directory environments.\n\nIt uses tickets to support authentication between users and services.\n\n### Simplified Kerberos authentication\n\n```text\n             USER\n              |\n              v\n      AUTHENTICATION SERVER\n              |\n              v\n       TICKET-GRANTING\n           TICKET\n              |\n              v\n       SERVICE REQUEST\n              |\n              v\n       SERVICE TICKET\n              |\n              v\n        TARGET SERVICE\n```\n\nThe user obtains tickets through the Kerberos infrastructure and presents an appropriate service ticket when accessing a network service.\n\nKerberos reduces the need to repeatedly send passwords across the network.\n\n### NTLM vs. Kerberos\n\n| NTLM | Kerberos |\n|---|---|\n| Challenge-response authentication. | Ticket-based authentication. |\n| Older Windows authentication mechanism. | Commonly used in Active Directory environments. |\n| Does not provide the same ticket-based mutual authentication model. | Supports mutual authentication in appropriate configurations. |\n| May be required for legacy compatibility. | Generally preferred in supported domain environments. |\n\nBoth protocols require secure configuration and monitoring.\n\n## 2.9 Windows Credential Protection\n\nWindows stores and processes authentication information using different mechanisms depending on the credential type.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Password-derived authentication material.\n- Kerberos tickets.\n- Credential Manager entries.\n- Windows Hello credentials.\n- Smart card credentials.\n\n### Credential Manager\n\nCredential Manager allows supported applications and Windows components to store and manage certain credentials.\n\nTo open it:\n\n1. Press `Windows + R`.\n2. Enter:\n\n```text\ncontrol /name Microsoft.CredentialManager\n```\n\n3. Press Enter.\n\nReview the saved credentials.\n\nDo not share screenshots containing usernames, server addresses or sensitive account information.\n\n### Windows Hello\n\nWindows Hello supports authentication using methods such as:\n\n- PIN.\n- Fingerprint.\n- Facial recognition.\n- Supported security devices.\n\nA Windows Hello PIN is generally associated with the specific device and protected by Windows security mechanisms.\n\nIt is not simply another version of an account password.\n\n## 2.10 Windows Security Policies\n\nWindows security policies define rules that control account behaviour and system security.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Password policies.\n- Account lockout policies.\n- User rights assignments.\n- Audit policies.\n- Security options.\n- Application restrictions.\n\nAdministrators can use Local Security Policy on supported editions.\n\nOpen it using:\n\n```text\nsecpol.msc\n```\n\nImportant sections include:\n\n| Section | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Account Policies | Password and account lockout settings. |\n| Local Policies | Audit policies, user rights and security options. |\n| Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security | Advanced firewall configuration. |\n| Restricted Groups | Controls group membership through policy. |\n| Application Control Policies | Helps restrict application execution. |\n\nOn Home editions, some management consoles may not be available.\n\n### Part 2 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- Windows security architecture.\n- Security Identifiers.\n- Access tokens.\n- Security descriptors.\n- DACLs and SACLs.\n- Security Reference Monitor.\n- Local Security Authority.\n- LSASS.\n- NTLM and Kerberos.\n- Credential protection.\n- Windows security policies.\n\n**Progress: Part 2 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 3: PRACTICAL INSPECTION OF WINDOWS ARCHITECTURE AND SECURITY COMPONENTS\n\nThis part introduces practical commands for examining the architecture and security configuration of an authorized Windows computer.\n\nOpen PowerShell. Use an administrator session only when a particular command requires elevated permissions.\n\n## 3.1 Practical 1: Identify Windows Architecture\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |\nSelect-Object Caption, Version, BuildNumber, OSArchitecture\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n`Get-CimInstance` retrieves information from Windows management interfaces.\n\n`Win32_OperatingSystem` represents operating system information.\n\n`Select-Object` selects the properties to display.\n\nThe selected properties include:\n\n- `Caption`: Windows product name.\n- `Version`: Operating system version.\n- `BuildNumber`: Windows build.\n- `OSArchitecture`: Operating system architecture.\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nCaption                   : Microsoft Windows 11 Pro\nVersion                   : 10.0.26100\nBuildNumber               : 26100\nOSArchitecture            : 64-bit\n```\n\nThe output will vary depending on your system.\n\n### Why is this useful?\n\nSecurity analysts need to know the operating system version before evaluating vulnerabilities, patch status and security features.\n\n## 3.2 Practical 2: Inspect the Running Kernel Version\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |\nSelect-Object Version, BuildNumber\n```\n\nWindows uses the NT kernel family.\n\nThe build number helps distinguish different Windows releases and update levels.\n\nYou can also run:\n\n```cmd\nver\n```\n\nThis displays Windows version information in Command Prompt.\n\nRemember that the version string alone is not a complete patch assessment.\n\n## 3.3 Practical 3: Inspect Running System Processes\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Process |\nSelect-Object ProcessName, Id, CPU |\nSort-Object CPU -Descending |\nSelect-Object -First 15\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- `Get-Process` retrieves running processes.\n- `Select-Object` chooses the process name, process ID and CPU information.\n- `Sort-Object CPU -Descending` sorts the processes by accumulated CPU time.\n- `Select-Object -First 15` displays the first fifteen results.\n\nThe CPU property generally represents accumulated processor time, not a live CPU percentage.\n\n### Identify important Windows processes\n\nExamples include:\n\n| Process | General Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| System | Core operating system activity. |\n| smss.exe | Session Manager Subsystem. |\n| csrss.exe | Client Server Runtime Subsystem. |\n| wininit.exe | Windows initialization process. |\n| services.exe | Service Control Manager process. |\n| lsass.exe | Local Security Authority Subsystem Service. |\n| winlogon.exe | Handles important interactive logon functions. |\n| svchost.exe | Hosts Windows services. |\n| explorer.exe | Windows shell and File Explorer. |\n\nSome processes can have multiple legitimate instances.\n\nDo not assume that a process is malicious simply because its name resembles a Windows system process.\n\n## 3.4 Practical 4: Inspect Process Executable Paths\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Process |\nWhere-Object { $_.ProcessName -eq \"lsass\" } |\nSelect-Object ProcessName, Id, Path\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- `Get-Process` retrieves processes.\n- `Where-Object` filters the results.\n- `$_` refers to the current object in the pipeline.\n- `ProcessName -eq \"lsass\"` selects the LSASS process.\n- `Select-Object` displays the selected properties.\n\nOn supported systems, the executable path should normally be associated with the Windows system directory.\n\nAccess to process paths may be restricted.\n\nFor important security investigations, verify executable signatures and other contextual evidence rather than relying on a filename alone.\n\n## 3.5 Practical 5: Inspect Windows Services\n\nA Windows service is a background component that can perform tasks without requiring a user to interact with it directly.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Windows Update services.\n- Network-related services.\n- Security services.\n- Printing services.\n- Database services.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Service\n```\n\nTo display running services:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Service |\nWhere-Object { $_.Status -eq \"Running\" }\n```\n\nTo inspect a specific service:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Service -Name WinDefend\n```\n\nThe service name and availability depend on the Windows installation and security configuration.\n\n### Inspect service startup configuration\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-CimInstance Win32_Service |\nSelect-Object Name, State, StartMode\n```\n\nThis displays service names, current states and startup modes.\n\n### Why is service inspection important?\n\nAttackers may attempt to maintain persistence through unauthorized services.\n\nSecurity analysts therefore investigate:\n\n- Newly created services.\n- Unexpected executable paths.\n- Unusual service accounts.\n- Unfamiliar service configurations.\n- Services that start automatically without a legitimate purpose.\n\nA disabled or stopped service is not automatically evidence of compromise.\n\n## 3.6 Practical 6: Inspect the Windows Registry\n\nThe Windows Registry is a hierarchical database that stores system and application configuration information.\n\nIt contains settings related to:\n\n- User accounts.\n- Installed software.\n- Hardware.\n- Windows services.\n- Security policies.\n- Startup configuration.\n- System behaviour.\n\n### Main Registry hives\n\n| Registry Hive | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM) | Computer-wide settings. |\n| HKEY_CURRENT_USER (HKCU) | Settings for the current user. |\n| HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT (HKCR) | File associations and COM-related information. |\n| HKEY_USERS (HKU) | Loaded user profiles. |\n| HKEY_CURRENT_CONFIG (HKCC) | Current hardware profile information. |\n\n### Opening Registry Editor\n\nPress:\n\n`Windows + R`\n\nEnter:\n\n```text\nregedit\n```\n\nPress Enter.\n\nYou may need administrative approval for certain operations.\n\n### Inspecting Windows Defender configuration\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-MpPreference |\nSelect-Object DisableRealtimeMonitoring, DisableBehaviorMonitoring\n```\n\nThis displays selected Microsoft Defender configuration settings.\n\nA returned value of `False` for these disable settings means the corresponding disable option is not set in that preference object.\n\nThe effective protection state should still be checked separately.\n\n### Registry security warning\n\nIncorrect registry modifications can:\n\n- Break applications.\n- Disable security features.\n- Cause system instability.\n- Prevent Windows from starting correctly.\n\nAlways understand a registry key before changing it.\n\nFor this lesson, inspect the registry without modifying protected system settings.\n\n## 3.7 Practical 7: Inspect Windows Security Identifiers\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /user\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /groups\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /priv\n```\n\nThese commands help you inspect:\n\n1. Your user SID.\n2. Your group memberships.\n3. Your available privileges.\n\n### Understanding the results\n\nSuppose your account belongs to the local Users group.\n\nThis indicates that the account has ordinary user group membership.\n\nIf it also belongs to the local Administrators group, it has administrative group membership, subject to UAC and the active security context.\n\nThese commands are particularly useful when troubleshooting access-denied errors.\n\n## 3.8 Practical 8: Inspect File and Folder Permissions\n\nCreate a test directory:\n\n```powershell\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsSecurityLab\"\n```\n\nCreate a test file:\n\n```powershell\nNew-Item -ItemType File -Path \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsSecurityLab\\report.txt\"\n```\n\nInspect its permissions:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Acl \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsSecurityLab\\report.txt\"\n```\n\n### Understanding the command\n\n- `New-Item` creates a new item.\n- `-ItemType Directory` creates a folder.\n- `-ItemType File` creates a file.\n- `$env:USERPROFILE` refers to your user profile directory.\n- `Get-Acl` retrieves the Access Control List and related security information.\n\n### Display individual access rules\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\n(Get-Acl \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsSecurityLab\\report.txt\").Access\n```\n\nThis displays the access rules associated with the file.\n\nYou may see information about:\n\n- IdentityReference.\n- FileSystemRights.\n- AccessControlType.\n- IsInherited.\n\n### Understanding permissions\n\n| Permission | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Read | Read file contents or list directory information, depending on the object. |\n| Write | Modify file contents or create entries in a directory, subject to other controls. |\n| Modify | Read, write and delete permissions within the applicable access rules. |\n| FullControl | Broad control over the object, including permission-management capabilities. |\n\nThe exact effective permissions depend on inherited rules, explicit rules, group memberships and Windows access-control evaluation.\n\n## 3.9 Practical 9: Inspect Windows Firewall Rules\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-NetFirewallRule |\nSelect-Object DisplayName, Enabled, Direction, Action |\nSelect-Object -First 20\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- `Get-NetFirewallRule` retrieves firewall rules.\n- `DisplayName` shows the rule name.\n- `Enabled` indicates whether the rule is enabled.\n- `Direction` identifies inbound or outbound traffic.\n- `Action` indicates whether traffic is allowed or blocked.\n- `Select-Object -First 20` limits the displayed results.\n\nTo inspect inbound rules:\n\n```powershell\nGet-NetFirewallRule -Direction Inbound |\nSelect-Object DisplayName, Enabled, Action |\nSelect-Object -First 20\n```\n\nFirewall rules may also be associated with application paths, ports, protocols, profiles and remote addresses.\n\nA rule being enabled does not necessarily mean it allows traffic. Review its action and conditions.\n\n## 3.10 Practical 10: Inspect Security Event Logs\n\nOpen Event Viewer:\n\n```text\neventvwr.msc\n```\n\nNavigate to:\n\n```text\nWindows Logs\n     |\n     +---- Security\n     |\n     +---- System\n     |\n     +---- Application\n```\n\nEach log serves a different purpose.\n\n| Log | Information |\n|---|---|\n| Security | Audited security activities. |\n| System | Operating system and service events. |\n| Application | Application-related events. |\n\n### Retrieve recent system events\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-WinEvent -LogName System -MaxEvents 10\n```\n\nThis displays the ten most recent system events.\n\n### Retrieve recent security events\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-WinEvent -LogName Security -MaxEvents 10\n```\n\nThis may require elevated permissions.\n\n### Why logs matter\n\nLogs help administrators reconstruct events and understand what happened before, during and after a security incident.\n\nHowever, logging must be appropriately configured. Not every activity is automatically recorded in every Windows installation.\n\n## 3.11 Practical 11: Inspect Windows Security Features\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_DeviceGuard `\n-Namespace root\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\n```\n\nThis can provide information about supported and configured Device Guard-related security features.\n\nDepending on hardware, Windows edition and configuration, the results may contain information about:\n\n- Virtualization-based security.\n- Credential Guard.\n- Hypervisor-protected Code Integrity.\n- Other device security capabilities.\n\nNot every feature is available on every computer.\n\nYou can also open:\n\n```text\nmsinfo32\n```\n\nLook for Device Guard and related security information in System Summary where available.\n\n## 3.12 Practical 12: Inspect Secure Boot Status\n\nSecure Boot helps protect the boot process by verifying that boot components are trusted according to platform security policy.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nConfirm-SecureBootUEFI\n```\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\nTrue\n```\n\nThis indicates Secure Boot is enabled.\n\nOther possible outcomes include:\n\n- `False`: Secure Boot is disabled.\n- An error: The system may not support the command, may be using legacy BIOS mode, or may not permit the query.\n\nDo not change firmware settings without understanding the consequences.\n\n## 3.13 Practical 13: Inspect Windows System File Integrity\n\nWindows includes System File Checker (SFC), which checks protected Windows system files and can repair certain integrity problems.\n\nOpen Command Prompt as Administrator.\n\nRun:\n\n```cmd\nsfc /scannow\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- `sfc` starts System File Checker.\n- `/scannow` instructs it to scan protected system files and attempt repairs when possible.\n\nPossible results include:\n\n- No integrity violations found.\n- Corrupted files found and successfully repaired.\n- Corrupted files found but some could not be repaired.\n\nSFC is useful for troubleshooting system-file integrity problems.\n\nIt is not a complete malware scanner.\n\nIf necessary, Windows also provides DISM to service and repair the Windows image.\n\nA common repair sequence is:\n\n```cmd\nDISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth\nsfc /scannow\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `DISM` runs the Deployment Image Servicing and Management tool.\n- `/Online` targets the currently running Windows installation.\n- `/Cleanup-Image` performs image servicing operations.\n- `/RestoreHealth` attempts to repair the component store.\n- `sfc /scannow` then checks protected system files.\n\n## 3.14 Practical 14: Understand Windows Boot Security\n\nWindows boot security includes multiple mechanisms that help protect the computer before the operating system fully starts.\n\nImportant components include:\n\n| Component | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| UEFI | Modern firmware interface used during startup. |\n| Secure Boot | Verifies trusted boot components according to firmware policy. |\n| TPM | Provides hardware-backed security capabilities. |\n| BitLocker | Encrypts supported storage volumes. |\n| Windows Boot Manager | Manages the Windows boot process. |\n| Windows Defender System Guard | Supports platform and boot integrity protections. |\n\n### Simplified boot process\n\n```text\n             POWER ON\n                 |\n                 v\n            UEFI FIRMWARE\n                 |\n                 v\n             SECURE BOOT\n                 |\n                 v\n        WINDOWS BOOT MANAGER\n                 |\n                 v\n          WINDOWS LOADER\n                 |\n                 v\n          WINDOWS KERNEL\n                 |\n                 v\n         WINDOWS SERVICES\n                 |\n                 v\n           SIGN-IN SCREEN\n```\n\nThis is a simplified conceptual representation. Actual boot stages and verification details depend on the platform and Windows configuration.\n\n### Part 3 Summary\n\nYou have practised:\n\n- Identifying Windows architecture.\n- Inspecting processes and services.\n- Examining executable paths.\n- Inspecting Registry configuration.\n- Reviewing SIDs and access tokens.\n- Checking NTFS permissions.\n- Inspecting firewall rules.\n- Reviewing Windows event logs.\n- Inspecting Device Guard features.\n- Checking Secure Boot.\n- Verifying system file integrity.\n- Understanding boot security.\n\n**Progress: Part 3 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 4: KAI ASSESSMENT, PRACTICAL PROJECT AND REVIEW\n\n## 4.1 Knowledge Assessment: Questions and Answers\n\n### Question 1\nWhat is Windows operating system architecture?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt is the internal organization of Windows components and how they work together to manage hardware, applications, processes, memory, storage and security.\n\n### Question 2\nWhat is the difference between user mode and kernel mode?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nUser mode provides restricted execution for ordinary applications and many processes. Kernel mode provides highly privileged execution for core operating system components and drivers.\n\n### Question 3\nWhat is the Windows kernel?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nThe kernel is the core operating system component responsible for low-level operations such as scheduling, interrupt handling and synchronization.\n\n### Question 4\nWhat is the Windows Executive?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt is a collection of kernel-mode components that provides services such as process management, memory management, object management and input/output management.\n\n### Question 5\nWhat is a system call?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA system call is a controlled mechanism through which an application requests a service from the operating system.\n\n### Question 6\nWhat is the purpose of the Hardware Abstraction Layer?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt helps Windows operate across supported hardware platforms by providing interfaces that isolate certain hardware-specific details.\n\n### Question 7\nWhat is a device driver?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA device driver is software that allows Windows to communicate with and manage a hardware device or software-defined device interface.\n\n### Question 8\nWhy are kernel-mode drivers security-sensitive?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nThey operate with high privileges. A vulnerable or malicious driver may provide opportunities for system-wide compromise.\n\n### Question 9\nWhat is a process?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA process is an instance of a running program that has its own operating system-managed resources and execution context.\n\n### Question 10\nWhat is a thread?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA thread is an execution unit within a process that is scheduled by the operating system to run on a processor.\n\n### Question 11\nWhat is virtual memory?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nVirtual memory provides processes with virtual address spaces that Windows maps to physical memory and, when needed, backing storage.\n\n### Question 12\nWhat is DEP?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nData Execution Prevention helps prevent code execution from memory regions designated as non-executable.\n\n### Question 13\nWhat is ASLR?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nAddress Space Layout Randomization makes important memory locations less predictable, making certain memory exploitation techniques harder.\n\n### Question 14\nWhat is an SID?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA Security Identifier is a unique identifier Windows uses to represent a user, group or other security principal.\n\n### Question 15\nWhat is an access token?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nAn access token represents a security context associated with a process or thread. It contains information such as user identity, group membership and privileges.\n\n### Question 16\nWhat is a security descriptor?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA security descriptor is a structure containing security information about a protected Windows object, including ownership, DACL and SACL information.\n\n### Question 17\nDifferentiate between DACL and SACL.\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA DACL determines which users or groups are allowed or denied access. A SACL defines which access events should be audited.\n\n### Question 18\nWhat is the Security Reference Monitor?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt is a Windows kernel-mode component responsible for enforcing access-control decisions.\n\n### Question 19\nWhat is LSASS?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nLSASS is the Local Security Authority Subsystem Service. It supports important authentication and local security-policy functions.\n\n### Question 20\nWhy is LSASS a security-sensitive process?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt handles sensitive authentication-related operations and information. Attackers may attempt to abuse access to it to compromise credentials or accounts.\n\n### Question 21\nWhat is the difference between Kerberos and NTLM?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nKerberos is a ticket-based authentication protocol commonly used in Active Directory environments. NTLM uses challenge-response authentication and is retained partly for compatibility with older systems.\n\n### Question 22\nWhat is the Windows Registry?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nThe Registry is a hierarchical database used to store Windows, hardware, user and application configuration information.\n\n### Question 23\nWhat is NTFS?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nNTFS is a Windows file system that supports features such as file permissions, ownership, auditing, journaling and other storage-management capabilities.\n\n### Question 24\nWhat is the purpose of Secure Boot?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nSecure Boot helps verify trusted boot components during startup according to the computer's firmware security policy.\n\n### Question 25\nWhat is the purpose of TPM?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA Trusted Platform Module provides hardware-backed security capabilities, such as protecting cryptographic material and supporting platform integrity measurements.\n\n### Question 26\nWhat does `whoami /user` display?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt displays the current user's account identity and Security Identifier.\n\n### Question 27\nWhat does `Get-Acl` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt retrieves security information, including access-control information, associated with a file-system object.\n\n### Question 28\nWhat does `Get-Service` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt retrieves information about Windows services, including their names and current status.\n\n### Question 29\nWhat is the purpose of SFC?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nSystem File Checker checks protected Windows system files and attempts to repair certain integrity problems.\n\n### Question 30\nWhy is defence in depth important in Windows security?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt combines multiple security controls so that if one protection fails, other controls can still reduce the likelihood or impact of compromise.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Practical Project: Windows Architecture and Security Investigation\n\n### Scenario\n\nFirebox Technologies has reported unusual activity on one of its Windows workstations.\n\nThe IT department wants you to perform an initial technical investigation.\n\nYou are assigned the role of a junior Windows security analyst.\n\nYour task is to inspect the operating system architecture, security identities, services, processes, permissions and security controls.\n\nYou must prepare an investigation report.\n\n### Project Objectives\n\nYou must:\n\n1. Identify the Windows operating system and architecture.\n2. Inspect running processes.\n3. Identify important Windows security processes.\n4. Inspect Windows services.\n5. Identify the current user's SID.\n6. Review group memberships and privileges.\n7. Inspect access permissions on a test file.\n8. Review Windows firewall rules.\n9. Examine security event logs.\n10. Check Secure Boot and available platform security features.\n11. Check Windows system file integrity.\n12. Recommend appropriate security improvements.\n\n### Step 1: Create Your Investigation Workspace\n\nOpen PowerShell.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsArchitectureLab\" -Force\n```\n\nThis creates your laboratory folder.\n\n### Step 2: Collect Operating System Information\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |\nSelect-Object Caption, Version, BuildNumber, OSArchitecture\n```\n\nRecord the operating system name, version and architecture.\n\nExplain why operating system information matters during vulnerability assessment.\n\n### Step 3: Inspect Important Windows Processes\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Process |\nWhere-Object {\n    $_.ProcessName -in @(\n        \"lsass\",\n        \"services\",\n        \"winlogon\",\n        \"csrss\",\n        \"smss\",\n        \"wininit\"\n    )\n} |\nSelect-Object ProcessName, Id, Path\n```\n\nIdentify the processes returned.\n\nFor each process, explain its general responsibility.\n\nDo not terminate or modify these critical system processes.\n\n### Step 4: Inspect Services\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-CimInstance Win32_Service |\nSelect-Object Name, State, StartMode, StartName\n```\n\nIdentify:\n\n- Running services.\n- Automatically started services.\n- Service accounts.\n\nInvestigate unfamiliar services without disabling them automatically.\n\n### Step 5: Inspect Security Identity\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /user\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /groups\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /priv\n```\n\nExplain what each output reveals about the current security context.\n\n### Step 6: Inspect File Permissions\n\nCreate a test file:\n\n```powershell\nNew-Item -ItemType File `\n-Path \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsArchitectureLab\\security-report.txt\"\n```\n\nInspect its permissions:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Acl \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsArchitectureLab\\security-report.txt\"\n```\n\nDisplay the access rules:\n\n```powershell\n(Get-Acl \"$env:USERPROFILE\\WindowsArchitectureLab\\security-report.txt\").Access\n```\n\nIdentify the owner, permissions and inheritance information.\n\n### Step 7: Inspect Firewall Rules\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-NetFirewallRule |\nSelect-Object DisplayName, Direction, Action, Enabled |\nSelect-Object -First 30\n```\n\nIdentify examples of inbound and outbound rules.\n\nExplain how these rules help control network communication.\n\n### Step 8: Review Security Events\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{\n    LogName = 'Security'\n    Id = 4625\n} -MaxEvents 10\n```\n\nReview available failed logon events.\n\nIdentify whether the results show any unusual patterns.\n\nDo not assume that every failed login represents malicious activity.\n\n### Step 9: Inspect Secure Boot\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nConfirm-SecureBootUEFI\n```\n\nRecord whether Secure Boot is enabled, disabled or unsupported.\n\nExplain how Secure Boot contributes to boot security.\n\n### Step 10: Inspect System File Integrity\n\nOpen Command Prompt as Administrator.\n\nRun:\n\n```cmd\nsfc /scannow\n```\n\nRecord the result.\n\nExplain what the tool checks and why it is useful during system troubleshooting.\n\n### Step 11: Prepare Your Security Findings\n\nUse this table:\n\n| Area | Observation | Security Importance | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Operating system | Your findings | Patch and compatibility assessment | Appropriate updates |\n| Processes | Your findings | Detect unusual execution | Investigate suspicious activity |\n| Services | Your findings | Identify unexpected services | Review service configuration |\n| User identity | Your findings | Understand privileges | Apply least privilege |\n| File permissions | Your findings | Prevent unauthorized access | Correct excessive permissions |\n| Firewall | Your findings | Restrict network traffic | Review firewall rules |\n| Security logs | Your findings | Detect unusual authentication | Investigate suspicious patterns |\n| Secure Boot | Your findings | Protect startup integrity | Enable where supported and appropriate |\n| System integrity | Your findings | Identify system-file problems | Repair verified integrity issues |\n\n### Step 12: Submit Your Investigation Report\n\nYour report should include:\n\n1. Introduction.\n2. Windows operating system information.\n3. Architecture observations.\n4. Important process findings.\n5. Service inspection results.\n6. User identity and privileges.\n7. File permission findings.\n8. Firewall configuration.\n9. Security log observations.\n10. Secure Boot status.\n11. System integrity results.\n12. Security recommendations.\n\nInclude screenshots of relevant commands and their outputs.\n\nRemove sensitive account information before sharing the report.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Lesson Completion Checklist\n\n- [ ] Explain Windows operating system architecture.\n- [ ] Differentiate between user mode and kernel mode.\n- [ ] Explain the Windows kernel.\n- [ ] Describe the Windows Executive.\n- [ ] Explain system calls.\n- [ ] Describe the Hardware Abstraction Layer.\n- [ ] Explain device drivers and driver security.\n- [ ] Differentiate processes and threads.\n- [ ] Explain virtual memory.\n- [ ] Understand DEP and ASLR.\n- [ ] Explain NTFS security.\n- [ ] Define Security Identifiers.\n- [ ] Explain access tokens.\n- [ ] Understand security descriptors.\n- [ ] Differentiate DACL and SACL.\n- [ ] Explain the Security Reference Monitor.\n- [ ] Describe LSASS and LSA.\n- [ ] Differentiate NTLM and Kerberos.\n- [ ] Understand the Windows Registry.\n- [ ] Explain Secure Boot and TPM.\n- [ ] Inspect processes and services using PowerShell.\n- [ ] Examine Windows file permissions.\n- [ ] Review security logs.\n- [ ] Complete the practical investigation project.\n\n## 4.4 Submission to Kai\n\nSubmit your Windows Architecture and Security Investigation report to Kai for marking.\n\nKai should evaluate:\n\n- Your understanding of Windows architecture.\n- Your ability to explain operating system components.\n- Your knowledge of Windows security mechanisms.\n- Your accuracy when using PowerShell.\n- Your ability to interpret security information.\n- Your ability to identify potential security weaknesses.\n- The quality of your recommendations.\n\n---\n\n# LESSON COMPLETE\n\n**Course:** Windows Security\n\n**Lesson:** Windows Operating System Architecture and Security Components\n\n**Level:** Beginner to Intermediate\n\n**Progress:** Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete\n\n**Next Lesson:** Windows User Accounts, Groups and Access Control";

export default windowsSecurityArchitectureComponentsLessonContent;
